  "read": {
    "headline": "The Fed is now 90% priced to hike, and the curve has already moved",
    "standfirst": "Core CPI came in hot on Friday and the market went from a coin flip to near-certainty on a September rise. The two-year has added 11% in a month while the thirty-year added under three — the whole board is downstream of that.",
    "points": [
      {
        "title": "The belly of the curve is where the repricing is happening.",
        "text": "Over the past month the one- to three-year part of the curve is up between 9.6% and 11.7%, while the twenty- and thirty-year are up 3.5% and 2.7%. Friday made it starker still: the two-year rose while the thirty-year fell. When the front leads by this margin the market is repricing the policy path, not the risk premium. It is also why 2s10s has flattened to 33bp from 40bp in two sessions, and why the twenty-year Treasury ETF is down over seven percent on the year while the one-to-three-year is down under two."
      },
      {
        "title": "Friday is a lesson in why this page prices everything in dollars.",
        "text": "Japan's Nikkei fell 1.93% on Friday. The dollar-denominated Japan ETF rose 2.20%. That four-point gap is not a data error — it is the yen, which strengthened into an expected Bank of Japan hike next week. A local index tells you what happened to the shares; the ETF tells you what happened to your account. If you hold unhedged foreign equity, the second number is the one that pays you."
      },
      {
        "title": "Energy finally broke, and only one thing broke with it.",
        "text": "Crude fell 2.4% on Friday on reports of a temporary Strait of Hormuz arrangement, after topping $100 midweek. Almost nothing else moved on that: the S&P rose 0.86%, the VIX fell 11%, and equities took the oil drop as relief. But heating oil is still up 123% on the year and diesel is at record highs, which is exactly the channel that fed Friday's hot core print. One down day in crude does not unwind a year of that."
      },
      {
        "title": "Value over growth is now an eighteen-point spread.",
        "text": "Value is 9.4% ahead of the S&P year to date and growth is 8.8% behind. That gap has widened every week this quarter and it is the same rates trade in equity clothing: long value against short growth is short duration. If you hold both and think of them as separate ideas, you are running one position twice and you will be right and wrong on the same days the bond market is."
      },
      {
        "title": "Asian semis and everything else are no longer the same asset class.",
        "text": "Korea is up 94% year to date and Taiwan 74.6%, while China is down 11.8%, India 10.1% and Indonesia 31.8%. The EM index is up 24%, which describes none of them. Note too that Korea and Taiwan both fell hard on Friday in local terms and still finished up in dollars — the same currency effect as Japan, running through the whole region."
      },
      {
        "title": "Gold did nothing all week, and that is the surprise.",
        "text": "A week with a hot PPI, a hot core CPI and a near-certain hike moved gold 0.68% lower over five days and left it up 0.50% on the year. It is down 12.9% over six months. A metal that is supposed to price monetary disorder sitting flat through a repricing this large is the cleanest disagreement on the board — either the rate market is overdoing the hikes, or gold has stopped listening."
      }
    ],
    "howToUse": "Do not trade the cells. Trade the disagreements between them. This week the loudest one is gold against the front end of the curve; last week it was the dollar. Everything here is a snapshot of Friday's close, not a live quote."
  },
